er's incumbency and money edge. The only polls are old and partisan-sponsored, so uncertainty stays high and the margin stays modest.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling and the News 12 debate could shift the race either way, especially if the aide-allegations story or the Golden endorsement moves swing voters.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 65 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mike Lawler (R) is running."]}, "overview": "NY-17 pairs first-term Republican incumbent Mike Lawler against Democrat Cait Conley in a Hudson Valley district that leans slightly Democratic. Lawler has a major financial edge, with more than $8 million raised and $4.8 million on hand, while Conley has raised $4.3 million and drawn about $3.2 million in combined outside support and opposition spending. Expert ratings are split: Cook, Decision Desk, Inside Elections, and Sabato call it a Tossup, while five other raters put it as Lean D or Likely D for a flip. Prediction markets on Kalshi, Polymarket, and PredictIt all price Conley around 65 percent. The only public polls date to July and come from opposing partisan sponsors, showing opposite five-point leads. A retiring Democratic congressman's endorsement of Lawler and an aide controversy add late noise to a genuinely competitive race.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7399, "margin": 4.04}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7185, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6936, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7094, "margin": 3.0}]}